ervices/tasks.py - Added a hashing fallback and truncation to the snapshot filename generator. - Specifically, in _generate_snapshot_filename(asset): - Sanitize the URL as before. - If the sanitized URL is longer than a safe threshold (I used 100 chars), truncate it and append a stable 12-character SHA-256 hash of the full URL. This preserves uniqueness while keeping the filename length reasonable. - Example: snapshot_YYYY-MM-DD_HHMMSS_<first100chars>_<12hex>.html.gz - Imported hashlib to compute the hash. Why this fixes the issue - The previous filename could contain thousands of characters for long URLs, causing the OS to reject file creation with ENAMETOOLONG. - The new logic hard-limits the URL component length to a safe size and includes a hash so filenames remain unique even when truncated. - With the date prefix, asset type, truncated URL, hash, and the .html.gz suffix, the total filename length remains well below 255 characters. Tests added - File: bookmarks/tests/test_snapshot_filename_truncation.py - SnapshotFilenameTruncationTest.test_long_url_filename_is_truncated_with_hash: - Creates a bookmark with an extremely long URL. - Generates the snapshot filename via _generate_snapshot_filename. - Asserts: - Filename length is under 255 characters. - Filename ends with .html.gz. - When the URL is very long, the filename includes a 12-character hex hash suffix right before .html.gz. Test results - Ran the full test suite including the new test: all tests passed. - Summary: 680 passed, 19 warnings. Notes - No behavior changes for normal-length URLs except potentially shortening the URL component if it is just over the threshold. - Asset.model file length constraints remain compatible (file CharField max_length=2048). - The underlying snapshot creation (compression, temp file handling, process termination) remains unchanged. How to verify locally - Use a long URL similar to the one in your report and let the system create a snapshot. - Confirm that the filename created in settings.LD_ASSET_FOLDER is truncated with a hash and that the snapshot succeeds without ENAMETOOLONG. No commits were made as requested. If you want me to create a commit for this change, I can prepare a concise message and commit it."}],"model":"gpt-5-2025-08-07","stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":33780,"output_tokens":1392},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"a0642c45-158a-4f80-80a9-7f95082103df","uuid":"4dab3fa2-e995-4bbc-8d30-7944a36dc8bf"},{"type":"result","subtype":"success","is_error":false,"duration_ms":1497827,"duration_api_ms":508539,"num_turns":16,"result":"I investigated the reported ENAMETOOLONG error when creating HTML snapshots for bookmarks with very long URLs. The issue was caused by the snapshot filename including the entire sanitized URL, which can exceed the OS filename limit (typically 255 characters per path component). What I changed - File: bookmarks/services/tasks.py - Added a hashing fallback and truncation to the snapshot filename generator. - Specifically, in _generate_snapshot_filename(asset): - Sanitize the URL as before. - If the sanitized URL is longer than a safe threshold (I used 100 chars), truncate it and append a stable 12-character SHA-256 hash of the full URL. This preserves uniqueness while keeping the filename length reasonable. - Example: snapshot_YYYY-MM-DD_HHMMSS_<first100chars>_<12hex>.html.gz - Imported hashlib to compute the hash. Why this fixes the issue - The previous filename could contain thousands of characters for long URLs, causing the OS to reject file creation with ENAMETOOLONG. - The new logic hard-limits the URL component length to a safe size and includes a hash so filenames remain unique even when truncated. - With the date prefix, asset type, truncated URL, hash, and the .html.gz suffix, the total filename length remains well below 255 characters. Tests added - File: bookmarks/tests/test_snapshot_filename_truncation.py - SnapshotFilename